<p class="gna-lede">Most people return to desk work or college one to two weeks after rhinoplasty, once the splint is off and the bruising has faded. To other people the nose usually looks presentable within about two to three weeks, but swelling settles slowly: most of the visible swelling goes in the first few months, while the tip can take a year or longer to reach its final shape. Everyone heals at their own pace, so treat the timeline below as a guide rather than a promise.</p>
<div class="gna-keypoints">
  <h3>Key points</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Swelling and bruising peak around days two to three, then steadily improve.</li>
    <li>The external splint, and any stitches that need removing, usually come off at about one week.</li>
    <li>Avoid blowing your nose, heavy lifting and resting glasses on the bridge until your surgeon says it is safe.</li>
    <li>The bridge settles first and the tip last; final refinement can take 12 months or more, longer with thick skin.</li>
    <li>Heavy bleeding, fever or severe pain inside the nose are reasons to call your surgeon promptly.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2 id="preparing">Preparing for a smoother recovery</h2>
<p>A good recovery starts before surgery. If you smoke, stopping for at least four weeks before and after the operation reduces the risk of poor healing. Tell your surgeon about every medicine and supplement you take: aspirin, some painkillers and supplements such as fish oil, vitamin E, garlic tablets and ginkgo can increase bleeding. Do not stop a prescribed blood thinner without your doctor's advice.</p>
<p>Arrange for someone to take you home and stay with you for the first night. Stock up on soft, easy-to-chew food, saline spray if advised, and an extra pillow or two. If you have a wedding, festival or important photographs coming up, allow at least three weeks of social recovery, and ideally several months if you want the nose to look settled in photographs.</p>

<h2 id="first-two-weeks">Day by day: the first two weeks</h2>
<h3>Day of surgery</h3>
<p>Rhinoplasty is done under general anaesthesia and usually takes one and a half to three hours. You wake with a splint on the outside of the nose and sometimes soft splints or light packing inside. Your nose will feel blocked, so you will breathe through your mouth. A small gauze pad under the nostrils catches oozing, which is normal for the first day or two. Many people describe the sensation as pressure and stuffiness rather than sharp pain, and it is usually controlled with the medicines prescribed. Some people stay in hospital for a night.</p>
<h3>Days 1 to 3</h3>
<p>Swelling and bruising increase and are usually at their worst around day two or three, often spreading under the eyes. Sleep and rest with your head raised on two or three pillows, use cold compresses on the cheeks and around the eyes (not on the splint), and keep the splint dry. Light bleeding and a blocked nose are expected. Short walks around the house help circulation; avoid bending, lifting and straining.</p>
<h3>Days 4 to 6</h3>
<p>Bruising begins to fade, turning yellow or green. Any internal packing is usually removed in the first few days, which often makes breathing a little easier. You may feel well enough to read, work on a laptop or watch films, but you will still tire easily. Continue to avoid blowing your nose; clean the nostrils gently as instructed and sneeze with your mouth open.</p>
<h3>Around day 7</h3>
<p>The external splint, and any stitches that are not dissolvable, are usually removed at about one week. This is an exciting moment, but expect the nose to look wider, fuller and perhaps slightly upturned, and the tip to feel stiff and numb. That is swelling, not your result.</p>
<h3>Days 8 to 14</h3>
<p>Most of the visible bruising fades over the second week, and many people return to desk work or classes, sometimes using light make-up to cover any remaining discolouration. The inside of the nose still feels congested as the lining heals, and crusts are common; saline sprays or rinses help if your surgeon recommends them.</p>

<h2 id="weeks-to-months">Weeks 2 to 12</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Weeks 2–3:</strong> bruising and redness have usually faded. You may be cleared to swim at around three weeks and to start light exercise.</li>
  <li><strong>Weeks 4–6:</strong> strenuous exercise can usually resume. If your nasal bones were reshaped, avoid resting spectacles on the bridge until about four to six weeks; lightweight frames taped to the forehead or contact lenses can bridge the gap.</li>
  <li><strong>Six weeks onwards:</strong> contact sports are often allowed only after six weeks or longer, depending on what was done.</li>
  <li><strong>Up to three months:</strong> breathing usually continues to improve, the sense of smell returns to normal if it was dulled, and the numbness of the tip gradually eases.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you ride a two-wheeler, wait until you can wear your helmet comfortably without pressure on the nose, and avoid dusty commutes for the first couple of weeks where you can.</p>

<h2 id="timeline-table">Recovery timeline at a glance</h2>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>When</th><th>What you may notice</th><th>What you can usually do</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Days 1–3</td><td>Peak swelling and bruising, blocked nose, light oozing</td><td>Rest with head raised, cold compresses, short walks indoors</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Days 4–7</td><td>Bruising fading, packing removed, splint off around day 7</td><td>Light activity at home, gentle nostril care</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Weeks 1–2</td><td>Most bruising gone, nose still swollen and stiff</td><td>Return to desk work or college for many people</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Weeks 3–6</td><td>Swelling noticeably less, tip still firm</td><td>Light exercise from about week 3, strenuous exercise from weeks 4–6</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Months 3–6</td><td>Shape clearer, occasional morning puffiness</td><td>Normal activities; follow-up reviews</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Months 12 and beyond</td><td>Tip refinement continues, especially with thicker skin</td><td>Final assessment; revision discussed only if needed</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

