<h2 id="swelling-guide">Swelling guide: why it lasts and how to help it settle</h2>
<p>Swelling lasts so long after nose surgery because the operation disrupts the tiny lymphatic channels that normally drain fluid from the skin. They regrow gradually over months. Until then, fluid lingers, most of all at the tip, where the skin is thickest and furthest from the main drainage routes.</p>
<h3>What is normal</h3>
<ul>
  <li>The bridge and upper nose settle first; the tip and the area just above it settle last.</li>
  <li>Swelling is often worse in the mornings and after heat, salty food, exercise or crying.</li>
  <li>One side may look more swollen than the other for weeks, which usually evens out.</li>
  <li>Thicker skin, which many Indian patients have, open rhinoplasty, reshaping of the bones, cartilage grafts and revision surgery all tend to mean longer swelling.</li>
</ul>
<h3>What helps</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Keep your head raised for the first one to two weeks, including during sleep.</li>
  <li>Use cold compresses around the eyes and cheeks for the first two to three days.</li>
  <li>Avoid heat on the face: hot showers directed at the face, steam, saunas and working over a hot stove. In a Hyderabad summer, stay in a cool room where possible.</li>
  <li>Eat less salt and avoid alcohol for at least the first two weeks; do not smoke.</li>
  <li>Protect your nose from the sun with a hat and sunscreen once the skin has healed, because bruised, swollen skin can darken with sun exposure.</li>
  <li>Use taping or gentle massage only if your surgeon recommends it.</li>
</ul>
<p>Some surgeons give a dose of steroid medicine during surgery. Studies suggest it can reduce swelling and bruising in the first few days, but its effect on longer-term swelling is less clear. Herbal remedies such as arnica are popular, but the evidence that they help is weak, so check with your surgeon before taking any supplement. Occasionally, stubborn swelling above the tip is treated with a small steroid injection months later; this is a specialist decision.</p>

<h2 id="warning-signs">Warning signs: when to call your surgeon</h2>
<div class="gna-callout gna-callout--warn">
  <p><strong>Contact your surgeon promptly, or go to the nearest emergency department or call 108, if you have:</strong> bleeding that soaks gauze repeatedly or does not settle after 15 minutes of sitting up, leaning forward and applying gentle pressure; a fever, spreading redness, worsening pain or foul-smelling discharge; severe pain and swelling inside the nose with both sides completely blocked, which can mean a blood collection on the septum; clear watery fluid dripping from the nose; any change in vision; or chest pain, breathlessness or a painful, swollen calf.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="risks-and-emotions">Risks and the emotional side of recovery</h2>
<p>Most recoveries are uneventful, but you should know the possible problems: bleeding, infection, prolonged swelling, numbness of the tip or upper front teeth, asymmetry, small irregularities that can be felt or seen, breathing difficulty, an altered sense of smell, and dissatisfaction with the result. A proportion of patients choose a revision operation, which is normally not considered until at least a year after surgery, when swelling has settled. Our article on <a href="/blogs/side-effects-of-maxillofacial-surgery-risks-complications-and-prevention/">the side effects of facial surgery</a> explains how these risks are reduced.</p>
<p>It is also very common to feel flat or anxious in the first weeks, when the nose looks swollen and others ask questions. This usually passes as the shape refines. Try not to examine your nose in the mirror every day, avoid comparing your early result with photographs of other people, and raise any worries at your follow-up visits rather than waiting.</p>
<p>If your operation also involved straightening the septum, you may find our guide to <a href="/blogs/septoplasty-vs-rhinoplasty-difference/">septoplasty vs rhinoplasty</a> helpful, and if your surgery followed an injury, see <a href="/blogs/broken-nose-when-does-it-need-surgery/">when a broken nose needs surgery</a>.</p>

<h2 id="when-to-see-specialist">When to see a specialist</h2>
<p>Recovery begins with a thorough consultation: an assessment of your breathing and nasal structure, a clear discussion of what can realistically be changed, and an honest timeline for your own circumstances. After surgery, follow-up visits are typically planned at about one week, one month and then at intervals over the first year, so that healing and breathing can be checked as the swelling settles.</p>
